ng, exemplified by the Esquiline Venus, caused a transformation in Roman artistic practices."}], "places": [{"name": "Rome, Italy", "role": "Cultural Center"}, {"name": "Capitoline Museums, Italy", "role": "Current Location"}, {"name": "Esquiline Hill, Italy", "role": "Discovery Site"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Esquiline Venus", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "esquiline-venus"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q2002838", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Esquiline_Venus"], "tags": ["statue", "Sculpture & Monuments", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "Key lasting impacts include: Influenced artistic traditions and cultural expression. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "The Esquiline Venus is significant for its role in shaping the artistic portrayal of femininity and beauty in Roman art, influencing both classical and modern perceptions of the female form.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-30T13:07:30Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#30545273640", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Roman nude marble sculpture at capitoline museums Created c. 50.", "newValue": "The Esquiline Venus, a striking Roman nude marble sculpture dating to approximately 50 CE, exemplifies the artistry and aesthetic values of the early Imperial period in Rome. Discovered on the Esquiline Hill, one of the famed Seven Hills of Rome, this work is housed today in the Capitoline Museums, providing insight into the cultural and artistic achievements of the time. The sculpture is not merely a representation of the female form; it symbolizes the Roman fascination with beauty, mythology, and the human body, resonating with the societal norms and values of the era.

